hi there Steve Kaufman here I want to  talk about and I this is connected with  of course the linguist on language blogs  I want to talk about an issue that's  come up in response to a video that I  did a little earlier where I said that  language learning is not about  performance and of course this has  attracted a lot of a lot but some  criticism from people who say that you  know performance is very important  people have come at me and said that you  know you are a dilettante this one I  don't always Harry or Billy or people  I've got commenting on my blog they're  saying that you know obviously my  knowledge I think was Harry said because  I only know 4,000 characters in Chinese  that Chinese people would respect me and  I butcher the language and all kind of  stuff like that how well do we need to  speak a language well it depends the  answer is it depends what you want to do  it's perfectly legitimate to just want  to take a peek at a language and see  what it's all about  just to get a little bit of a sense of  the language just because you're  interested perfectly legitimate you may  want to learn enough in order to order a  beer and find the bathroom I personally  don't do that because I find that you  know if I go to a place with a few  phrases I'm pretty soon overwhelmed with  whatever the native comes back to me  with so so my own preference in learning  is to get to a point where I can  communicate comfortably on a fairly wide  range of subjects I also find that  learning a language is a sort of I often  say it's it's a it's a  the famous myth of sisyphus and i can't  remember whether he was the one pushing  the rock up the hill that kept on  rolling down or he was in a cave and the  water lever level kept on rising I can  one of these Greek myths the point is  you'll never achieve perfection no  matter how long you spend at it you'll  always wish you were a little better so  given that that's the case you may as  well enjoy whatever level you have and  if you want to stop there that's your  choice so it's not for others to  criticize your level in the language  language learning to me should be a  personal thing something you do for your  own enjoyment now granted there may be  situations for school or to get a job or  you have to achieve a certain level of  fluency and typically of course this  level of fluency is measured in some  kind of grammar test or whatever but  even there I'm convinced that people who  approach language learning with  intention of enjoying it and exposing  themselves to a lot of content listening  and reading and enjoying it and  communicating with people without  worrying it worrying about it those  people will do better and I've mentioned  before that in Canada when they because  they have a bilingual fullness they'll  test Anglophones on their french and if  they can't use the subjunctive correctly  then they don't get their Frank French  your bonus or they don't qualify as  bilingual which of course is just  typically ridiculous ridiculous there  are lots of people who won't get the  subjunctive right and who can  communicate very effectively and who are  not afraid to use the language and there  will be those who will pass their test  on the subjunctive and won't open their  mouth because they're so concerned about  speaking perfectly so to my mind how  well do we speak the language  do we need to speak it as well as you  want do we need to be perfect in order  to say were fluent no we need to be able  to communicate comfortably  and we ourselves are the best judge of  when we are comfortable in a language  and so therefore I'm against people  competing in their performance this  idiot and I'll say he's an idiot this  hairy guy telling me the unit you need  to have 8,000 characters and that I'm  butchering the language I don't care  Harry how well you speak Chinese and you  shouldn't care how well I speak Chinese  and this gets back to this other issue  of a remark that was attributed to cap  sumoto who is a very well known figure  intern in language learning circles on  the web and who has is approach to  language learning much of which I agree  with the idea that we should mind the  language itself for things that we need  he emphasizes of mining it for sentences  I don't agree I find it sentences are  all somewhat unique I prefer to mine  content from words and phrases but we  quibble  fundamentally it's the language that  teaches us but katsumoto's supposed to  have said and I haven't heard this from  him so I can't say that he did say it's  just attributed to him supposed to have  said that he doesn't like people who  claim to speak many languages because  they don't speak any of them well and he  finds it very unsatisfying to talk to  someone who doesn't have this depth in  the language well I don't I don't know  why they cut some would have said this  but if that was what said I mean I don't  I learned a language Japanese French  Spanish Chinese Russian mostly to  communicate with speakers of the  language not to communicate with other  learners so I communicate with native  speakers when I read and listen which is  what I mostly do because I don't know  many Russians here but if I meet a  Russian then I communicate with him or  her in Russian and if we are successful  in communicating I'm happy whether that  native speaker is interested in getting  to know me for whatever reason  including my ability in the language  well that's his choice or hers but for  us to say we don't respect other  language learners because they haven't  achieved a certain depth in our language  to me that's just silly what we do with  our languages is entirely our own affair  so I'm against the idea of perfection  I'm against the idea that we're  constantly worried about how well we  speak language learning is a personal  thing and and so far speaking with other  non-native speakers I have no interest  in speaking with other non-native  speakers and I would suggest that for  learners of language the further away  they get from other non-native speakers  without better especially from their own  language group and I think you know  whether it be in Japan or France or  places like that where people sit in  class or ting in an english-speaking  countries they sit in class with other  people who speak their own language and  hear them but there we can talk about  that wouldn't say much of the language  but that's not what you want to emulate  so you should really focus on listening  to the language spoken by native  speakers and but not pass judgment on  other people's learning efforts  everybody learns for their own reasons  everybody should feel satisfied with  whatever level they have achieved and if  they're motivated to do do more they  should continue and they will never  achieve perfection and so they may as  well enjoy it so there you have it
 

